    title="The filpgf.io roadmap"
    intro="What we’re building into the Filecoin PGF portal, who is building it, and when each piece ships."
  />

  <div class="container-page pb-16 sm:pb-24">
    <div class="prose-post max-w-3xl">
      <p>
        filpgf.io is becoming the place where the Filecoin Public Goods Funding
        (PGF) program shows its work: what’s funded, how those projects are
        doing, and how decisions get made. This page covers our objectives, the
        features that deliver them and the teams building them, how funding
        support comes together, and the roadmap for when each piece ships. For a
        shorter version, see the companion blog post.
      </p>

      <p><strong>On this page</strong></p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#building-toward">What we’re building toward</a></li>
        <li><a href="#building-with">Who we’re building with</a></li>
        <li>
          <a href="#features">The features, up close</a>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="#impact-tracking">Impact and maintenance tracking</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="#ai-native">AI-native applications and reviews</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="#admin-views">Admin views and funding workflows</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#funding-support"
            >Funding support: faster decisions, fewer nudges</a
          >
        </li>
        <li><a href="#ships">When each feature ships</a></li>
        <li><a href="#adds-up">What this adds up to</a></li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="building-toward">What we’re building toward</h2>
      <p>
        The build is organized around three objectives: understand what funding
        produces, make applications and reviews faster and lighter, and give the
        people running the program one place to see and act on everything. Each
        one comes with a set of features, built together with our partners
        <a href="https://www.karmahq.org/">Karma</a>,
        <a href="https://www.oso.xyz/">OSO</a>, and
        <a href="https://www.simocracy.org/">Simocracy</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="eyebrow">OBJECTIVE 1</p>
      <h3>Measure impact and maintenance</h3>
      <p>
        Funding only matters if what it produces lasts. OSO tracks impact and
        maintenance for every funded project across
        <a href="https://www.filpgf.io/kernel/">Kernel</a>,
        <a href="https://www.filpgf.io/rnd/">R&amp;D</a>, and
        <a href="https://www.filpgf.io/revenue-development/"
          >Revenue Development</a
        >, and is being built out to flag stalled data sources before anyone has
        to go looking. Verified outcomes will also be captured as project-level
        hypercerts.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Includes:</strong> Metrics on cover pages and reports · Automated
        health monitoring · Hypercerts on verified outcomes
      </p>

      <p class="eyebrow">OBJECTIVE 2</p>
      <h3>Make applications and reviews AI-native</h3>
      <p>
        Applications adapt to each project’s scope of work, and a Simocracy sim
        drafts the first review of every milestone. A person always makes the
        call, and every review is captured in filpgf.io.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Includes:</strong> Adaptive applications · Sim-assisted milestone
        review · Human sign-off
      </p>

      <p class="eyebrow">OBJECTIVE 3</p>
      <h3>Track every funded project, keep ops light</h3>
      <p>
        Karma gives the program team one admin view of every application,
        milestone, and invoice, queued by priority and age. Feedback from Slack,
        Telegram, and Simocracy attaches to the item it belongs to, so there’s
        less chasing for everyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Includes:</strong> Priority and age queues · Slack and Telegram feedback
        · SLA reporting
      </p>

      <p>
        <em
          >Underneath all three: one source of truth for the program, so funded
          teams, reviewers, and the program team all work from the same
          information.</em
        >
      </p>

      <p class="eyebrow">WHO WE’RE BUILDING WITH</p>
      <h2 id="building-with">One loop, three partners</h2>
      <p>
        These pieces are being built to work together. Applications, reviews,
        and impact data all feed one system, so what we learn at each step
        informs the next. Every time the loop comes around, the next review
        starts with better data than the last one, and the same process helps us
        efficiently track and showcase the ecosystem’s needs and priorities, and
        keep applicants and stakeholders informed about them.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Partner</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
            <th>Next in the loop</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1. Karma</strong><br />HOSTS &amp; SURFACES</td>
            <td>
              Holds the program’s records: incoming applications and milestones,
              plus the reviews and metrics the loop produces, surfaced in admin
              queues, cover pages, and Ask Karma.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Syncs</strong> applications &amp; milestones → Simocracy
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Simocracy</strong><br />REVIEW</td>
            <td>
              A sim drafts the first assessment of every application and
              milestone. A person reviews it and makes the call.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Informs</strong> funded projects to track → OSO</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>3. OSO</strong><br />MEASURE</td>
            <td>
              Tracks impact and maintenance for every funded project, and flags
              a stalled data source before anyone goes looking.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Feeds back</strong> impact &amp; maintenance metrics → Karma
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        <em
          >At the center: filpgf.io · one shared record. Everything each partner
          produces lands in the same place.</em
        >
      </p>

      <p class="eyebrow">FEATURE SPOTLIGHTS</p>
      <h2 id="features">The features, up close</h2>
      <p>
        Each objective comes down to a set of features. Here is what each one
        does, what it looks like, and who is building it: OSO on impact and
        maintenance data, Simocracy on AI-native applications and milestone
        review, and Karma on the platform and admin views that tie everything
        together.
      </p>

      <p class="eyebrow">FEATURE AREA 1</p>
      <h3 id="impact-tracking">Impact and maintenance tracking</h3>
      <p>Kernel · R&amp;D · Revenue Development</p>
      <p>Built with <a href="https://www.oso.xyz/">OSO</a></p>
      <p>
        Funding a project is one decision. Knowing whether it keeps delivering
        takes ongoing measurement. In filpgf.io, every funded project gets two
        things kept current: <strong>impact</strong> (what the work is producing)
        and <strong>maintenance</strong> (whether it is still actively built and cared
        for). The approach runs across all three funding categories, with one addition
        for RevDev: a revenue-linked metric such as paid usage, onchain revenue, or
        paying customers.
      </p>

      <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-tint p-6">
        <p class="eyebrow">THE TEAM</p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://www.oso.xyz/">Open Source Observer (OSO)</a> is an AI-native
          data platform that pulls scattered signals into one semantic layer, so people
          and agents work from the same source of truth. Its core measurements, such
          as monthly active developers, builder retention, and new developer acquisition,
          are how open source ecosystems tell a thriving project from one that has
          gone quiet.
        </p>
      </div>

